course: dictionary
lesson: 1677
title: Chopsticks and context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A chopstick is one of a pair of thin sticks used to pick up food and eat it. You can work out a word like this from the words around it: «Chopsticks are never stuck upright in rice» tells you they are used with food, at meals.
explain: Look at the clues in «It is polite to try chopsticks, and it is fine to ask for a fork.» The words polite and fine show that using chopsticks is a matter of manners, and that a fork is a good alternative. Ask yourself: what would fit here? A thin stick for picking up food fits.
explain: Notice that chopsticks come in a pair. «A boy in Leeds is given chopsticks at a friend's dinner» shows they are used at meals even far from East Asia. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: A chopstick is one of a pair of thin sticks used to pick up and eat food.
fact: Chopsticks are used mainly in East Asian countries like China, Japan and Korea.
fact: We say «chopsticks» in the plural because they usually come as a pair.
fact: «Never stand chopsticks upright in rice» because in some Asian cultures it looks like a funeral custom.
fact: If chopsticks are hard to use, it is polite to ask for a fork.
exercise: «Chopsticks are never stuck upright in rice.» What is a chopstick? | A type of noodle | *One of a pair of thin sticks used to pick up food | A small bowl for rice | A fork with two prongs
exercise: «It is polite to try chopsticks, and it is fine to ask for a fork.» What should you do if chopsticks are hard to use? | Stand them upright in your rice | *Politely ask for a fork | Leave the table without a word | Point and wave them at your friend
exercise: In which country are chopsticks mainly used? | *Japan | Egypt | Canada | Brazil
test: «A boy in Leeds tries them, then politely asks for a fork.» What does politely mean here? | Rudely and loudly | Quickly and quietly | Angrily | *In a kind, well-mannered way
test: Why should you never stand chopsticks upright in rice? | *In some Asian cultures it looks like a funeral custom | Chopsticks break in rice | It makes the rice too hot | Rice is too sticky to hold them
exam: Which phrase means using chopsticks at a meal? | *Eat with chopsticks | Lift a plate | Stand a spoon | Wave a fork
